and in the aftermath of the deadliest  mass shooting in modern US history we're  focusing on some of the people who stood  up and helped others that night one of  them is a US Marine veteran his name is  Taylor Winston instead of leaving the  scene to escape the gunfire  he helped victims and drove more than 20  of them to the hospital  Noro Donald's with us once again from  Las Vegas with his story Nora thank  goodness there are always stories like  this in times like this Gail you are  absolutely right we are reminded of the  humanity really good people as well that  do so much in the face of such tragedy  and the man we met last night had  remarkable poise less than 24 hours  after the attack and Taylor Winston told  us how he loaded some of the most  critical victims into a stolen truck he  stole the truck and then he sped it to  Desert Springs hospital Medical Center  all before ambulances had arrived on the  scene a friend got a few drinks and we  found our other friends near the side  stage and we just kind of posted up  there to watch and enjoy the concert  Taylor Winston and girlfriend Jen Lewis  were dancing the two-step stage right  moments later the gunfire began people  start scattering and screaming and  that's when we knew something real was  happening Winston and Lewis and  thousands of others needed to get to  safety but were boxed in by a fence the  shots got louder and louder closer just  and saw people getting hit it was like  we could be hit in any second once we  got to the fence helped throw a bunch of  people over and got myself over there's  a mini war zone but we couldn't fight  back the 29 year old veteran couldn't  fight the threat but he was able to  drive I saw a field with a bunch of  white trucks I tested my luck to see if  any of them had keys in it and first one  that we tried opening had keys sitting  right there and started looking through  people to take to the hospital there was  just too many and it was overwhelming  how much blood was everywhere victims  squeezed into the backseat and spread  across the bed of the truck  once we dropped him off we were like  let's go back for round two and go get  some more I transported probably 20 or  30 people injured to  Hospital Winston joined the Marines at  age 17 eventually serving two tours in  Iraq in 2011 he was honorably discharged  as a sergeant I think a lot of my  training in the military helped me in  the situation we needed to get them out  of there regardless of our safety  Winston rejects the hero label he said  he saw many people like him doing good  deeds there was a lot of bravery being  courageous people out there and I'm glad  that can call them my country folk just  think about that I mean Winston says  he's just 100% lucky that he was not  injured or killed many returned the keys  to the owner of the truck last night  that he stole it was all worth it  Winston says he doesn't know which of  his passengers survived but he feels  confident that his decision made a big  difference
 

